From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(input): apply server movement and camera permissions

Consume the core versioned permission channel for direct and ViaProxy connections. Filter gameplay key samples and recalculate the normalized movement vector before Java prediction. Camera locks discard mouse look while preserving menu controls and clearing smoothing history.

Send physical gameplay samples before local filtering only when the server advertises the raw-input channel. Suppress identical samples and resend the initial state on a new connection. This preserves Bedrock Script API input during movement locks without sending custom packets to ordinary Java servers or older core builds.

Clear permissions and sample history on remote disconnect and local world exit. Preserve held key state so controls resume when the server unlocks them. Manual mounting and broader vehicle comparisons remain incomplete.

Resolve the current permitted keyboard sneak button during Java crouch selection. Java 26.3 checks crouching before refreshing KeyboardInput, so the first predicted step retained walking speed. Strict BDS 1.26.51.1 (protocol 2193) corrected this transition three times in each recorded repeat: Java predicted 0.098 blocks while BDS expected 0.0294. Keep Java pose eligibility and speed attributes, and activate the change through the connection-scoped core channel so direct and ViaProxy routes share it. Preserve other input implementations and ordinary Java connections.

Retain the previous pressed sample for the release frame. A continuous-walk capture shows BDS expects a final slow step on StopSneaking; applying full walking speed immediately caused two additional corrections. The hook preserves Java pose eligibility and other input implementations rather than replacing movement simulation.

Enable the existing Bedrock fluid interaction and player water hooks from the connection-scoped core snapshot as well as the direct protocol selection. ViaProxy negotiates Java 26.3 with the client, so a protocol-only guard left it using Java fluid collision bounds and water prediction. Strict BDS 1.26.51.1 recorded ten moving prediction corrections during a held-rise case through ViaProxy, while the same direct case had none. Keep the existing fluid formulas and early direct activation; remote disconnect and local world exit clear the snapshot. Direct-only auth-input tracking still requires a local core connection.

Clear the existing raw-input dedup sample on a permission snapshot and
on an observed unavailable transport or connection. When eligibility
returns, the unchanged held physical input must send once before normal
dedup resumes. Core capability withdrawal already restores standard
input; keeping an old client sample would otherwise suppress that fresh
physical state.

Private actual callback and joined packet controls cover this transition.
Their Mockito fixtures remain private because that test dependency is
introduced by a later owner; no dependency or transport format moves.
